The '''infosphere''' is a philosophical concept developed by Luciano Floridi concerning informational environments, their entities, processes and relations. It has a narrower sociotechnical interpretation and a more expansive philosophical interpretation. Which sense is intended matters for evaluating both descriptive and normative claims.

== Scope and development ==
In “A Look into the Future Impact of ICT on Our Lives” (2007), Floridi examines the changing distinction between online and offline environments and proposes that people increasingly inhabit a shared informational environment with engineered artifacts. ''The Fourth Revolution'' (2014) develops this argument about human self-understanding. These texts are philosophical analyses and forecasts; particular changes in privacy or institutional behavior require independent empirical evidence.

A narrow interpretation treats an infosphere as an environment in which information is produced, accessed, processed or exchanged. In the broader interpretation, the world is approached through informational entities and processes. Representing a physical entity in an information model does not establish that it is ontologically identical to that representation.

== Levels of abstraction ==
Floridi's method of levels of abstraction (2008) provides a way to state which observable features and types a model retains. A clinic, for instance, might be modeled at one level by patient identifiers and record-access events and at another level by embodied interactions, clinical judgments and institutional rules. The levels retain different properties and enable different questions.

As an illustrative model, specify an observable set <math>O=\{o_1,\ldots,o_n\}</math> and a mapping from system states to observations <math>f:S\to O_1\times\cdots\times O_n</math>. If two states have the same observed values, the model cannot distinguish them at that level; this is a limitation of the chosen representation, not proof of their metaphysical identity. This notation is an example of operational modeling, not a quotation or formal definition from Floridi.

== Inforgs ==
Floridi's term ''inforg'' characterizes humans as informational organisms connected with other entities and artifacts in an informational environment. It is an interpretation of identity and agency in technologically mediated life. In his 2007 article, the term contrasts with the image of a cyborg whose body has been technologically modified: pervasive informational integration need not involve physical augmentation. The concept is distinct from the technical notion of an [[Intelligent agent/Technical|intelligent agent]], which concerns specified perception, action and objectives. It does not independently establish artificial consciousness or moral status.

== Information ethics and philosophical disagreement ==
In ''The Ethics of Information'' (2013), Floridi develops an ontocentric ethical approach that considers informational entities as possible recipients of harm and argues for an account extending beyond exclusively human-centered ethics. This is a normative proposal, not a conclusion derived from Shannon entropy or computational functionality. The 2014 Searle–Floridi exchange illustrates disagreement over whether the informational description yields a substantive account of reality and human identity. Distinguish methodological usefulness, claims about ontology and the justification of moral obligations.

== Operational application and measurement ==
A bounded informational-environment study could represent entities, access decisions and provenance using a typed graph. For a data-sharing system, define an authorized actor–resource relation, time-indexed access events and a record of transformations. Test access-control behavior, completeness of provenance and correction propagation under defined scenarios. An authorization graph is not a measure of all interactions; physical and undocumented events may be absent. Auditing these features evaluates the engineered system, not the metaphysical proposition that reality is fundamentally informational.

[[Information ecology/Technical|Information ecology]] provides a complementary sociotechnical perspective focused on situated human practices and information management. The [[Information theory/Technical|mathematical theory of information]] provides quantifiable measures for specified probabilistic systems, not a general test of semantic, ontological or ethical status.

